<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Every user-visible string in the app.

    This file previously held exactly one entry (app_name); all other copy was a
    Kotlin literal, which meant the app could not be translated at all and there
    was no single place to review its voice.

    Two conventions:

    - The dystopian register belongs to *chrome* — status lines, section headings,
      button labels, the audit log. Anything that tells the user something factual
      about their food, their data, or a failure is written plainly. "Character in
      the chrome, clarity in the data."
    - ALL CAPS is for short system labels only. Sentences are sentence case:
      all-caps prose measurably slows reading and is announced awkwardly by screen
      readers.

    Internal diagnostics (log tags, audit categories) are deliberately NOT here.
    They are never shown to a user and translating them would only make crash
    reports harder to read.
-->
